On 2008-12-07, Brian M. Dean <elzoog@yahoo.com> wrote:
> Basically what this does is take your save file and copy it to a 
> different directory.  If you die, it will then take your old save file 
> and put it back in your regular save directory.  Thus what you are 
> effectively doing is restoring from a previous save.

So how is this any different than playing in eXplore mode and taking
advantage of the "Do you want to keep the save file? [yn] (n)"
feature?

Your script doesn't easily allow for multiple save files, which would
be a new feature (if you were to write such a script, I would suggest
it be used with eXplore mode to keep the distinction between learning
about the game and playing "for real" clear)

> 1) Nethack already has a feature called "explore mode" where you can 
> choose whether you want to die or not.

(...which already provides this feature as well)

> I would be interested in why you guys think that doing this is wrong.

I don't think it's wrong, I just think it's not the same game as the
rest of us are playing.  While I believe you make a good point that
having the option of retaining a saved game to explore alternative
outcomes to a situation can be a good way to learn more about the
game, I believe this style of play belongs firmly where it already is,
in "non-scoring discovery mode" (aka explore mode).

I think other posters are correct that when you play with recoverable
saves (or Die?  (y/n)), you fail to develop a certain set of skills
which are helpful to playing NetHack well--those related to carefully
considering moves to avoid an unrecoverable error.

I recommend to people learning the game that they should occasionally
play games in (or switch to) "explore mode" when they want to learn
more about how the game works, but that they should also play "the
real game" regularly to remember that carelessness can kill,
permanently.

P.S. As demonstrated elsewhere, I did not know explore mode featured
save preservation until this thread.  I appreciate that your post
brought this information to my attention.


